B2.notes:
Control/data flow: Ctrl+P -> dialogStore.openQuickOpen() -> +page.svelte passes open=true to the always-mounted QuickOpen.svelte. Typing fires handleInput() which, after a 50ms debounce, reads the search root from getCwdPath() = windowTabsManager.getActiveExplorer().currentPath, sets up a 'search-results' listener, and calls startStreamingSearch(query, cwd, 20). The Rust backend (search.rs) walks the tree on a std::thread and emits batched 'search-results' events tagged with a monotonic searchId (task_registry.rs). QuickOpen filters events two ways: (1) searchGeneration closure guard, incremented per debounce/keystroke; (2) activeSearchId, which it either learns from startStreamingSearch's return or ADOPTS from the first arriving event when still null.

Root cause (verified in code): searches are keyed to a query generation and a backend searchId, but NOT to the directory they were issued for, and the guard is never re-armed across dialog close/reopen (searchGeneration is component-scoped state that persists because the component stays mounted). The concrete leak: after navigating from dir A to dir B and reopening/typing, a still-running A-rooted backend search (cancellation is cooperative and only checked between batches in search.rs) can emit a batch into the newly created generation-matching listener before startStreamingSearch(B) resolves. With activeSearchId===null, setupSearchListener adopts A's searchId (lines 225-226) and renders A's results — 'results from the previously visited directory.' Directory-independent frecency/recent matches are not the culprit; the leaked rows are backend fuzzy results whose paths sit under the old root.

Most likely fix site: QuickOpen.svelte alone. Viable approaches (precision note — all are single-file except (b)): (a) capture the cwd at issue time and discard events not belonging to the current cwd/generation, and/or bump ++searchGeneration + null activeSearchId in the close and open $effects so orphaned searches can never match; (b) stop 'adopting' an unknown searchId — only accept events once startStreamingSearch has returned the concrete id, which additionally requires threading the search root into SearchResultsEvent (touches src/lib/api/search.ts + src-tauri/src/search.rs) if you want positive root verification rather than just id/generation matching. Route (a) is the minimal correct fix; route (b) is the more defensive cross-layer fix."}
